Record one line per demo and panel: `demo | output coordinate | panel bounds | background/divider/anomaly colors | anomaly coordinates/count | output value`.

Check whether the output matches a feature of the panel (presence, count, parity, location, shape) and replay that feature across every example. Include empty panels and panels with repeated anomalies; these distinguish binary presence from count-sensitive rules. Keep output shape inference separate from value inference. After a wrong submission, mark the rejected hypothesis explicitly and restart comparison rather than changing only the output palette.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-integration/references/panel-hypothesis-ledger.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/post-error-evidence-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Post-error evidence checkpoint

After an incorrect submission, treat the feedback as falsifying the submitted candidate as a whole. Re-read all input and demos and identify which assumptions may be wrong: output scope/dimensions, panel pairing, value mapping, trigger condition, or exceptional cells. Do not respond with cosmetic variants (for example, switching between a uniform background and that background plus one guessed marker) unless a complete demo-validated rule supports the change.

For divider-separated panels, explicitly test aligned cell-pair mappings across every demo coordinate and account for all output values, including rare exceptions. If the mapping or operation is still ambiguous, request a new demo chosen to distinguish named alternatives; do not spend another submission on an ungrounded guess. Validate the transferred test output's dimensions and all cells before submitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/post-error-evidence-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/demo-integration-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Demo integration checkpoint

After each returned Continual-ARC demonstration, reconcile the new pair against the complete existing ledger before taking another action. Do not request another demo reflexively. State which competing hypotheses remain and what observable structure in a new pair would discriminate them.

When output dimensions are exactly twice the input in each axis, do not infer simple tiling from dimensions alone. Compare all four output quadrants/translated blocks with the input and plausible rotations or reflections; validate each cell, including boundaries, against every available pair. For heterogeneous-looking pairs, seek a common input-driven generator that explains all demonstrations rather than discarding the outlier. Request further evidence only for a concrete ambiguity with meaningful expected information.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/demo-integration-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/repeated-pattern-misdirection.md", "content": "# Periodic output versus input-driven transformation

When several examples show periodic full-grid outputs, first verify whether the output is generated solely from a fixed palette cycle or whether it is a transformation of the input's marker arrangement. Record the complete mapping from each input row/cell to the corresponding output, including locations where the input is blank and output is nonblank. A small demo with distinctive sparse markers can reveal an operation such as filling a sequence, shifting or cycling colors, or extending a pattern; do not ignore it because other examples are dense stripes.

After a periodic-pattern submission fails, reject that generator as a whole unless it can be reconciled with every demo. Do not spend further submissions on arbitrary phase or palette variants. Use remaining demos to resolve explicit alternatives (e.g. row-wise versus column-wise sequence, marker-order preservation versus fixed cycle), then validate the candidate on all known examples before resubmitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/repeated-pattern-misdirection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/pair-mapping-validation.md", "content": "# Pair-mapping validation for split panels

When the input has two noisy panels separated by a divider and the output is smaller, treat aligned positions in the panels as paired observations. From every demo, build a full contingency table `(left_value, right_value) -> output_value`, not a handful of examples. Check consistency for every observed pair, note any exceptions, and ensure output dimensions correspond to one panel's aligned positions. This is especially important when demos vary in palette: infer the relational mapping from each full example before transferring it to a test palette.

Do not derive test outputs by recoloring both input cells independently, selecting whichever input color seems dominant, or guessing from apparent color similarity. Those approaches can produce plausible but wholly incorrect grids. If one or more pair types are not resolved, request a demo only when it is likely to expose those pair types; otherwise, do not spend attempts on unverified mappings. Validate the completed grid's dimensions, row count, and values before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/pair-mapping-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/demo-chain-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Demo-chain checkpoint

After each requested demonstration returns, integrate it before choosing another action. Record its dimensions, input/output relationship, preserved and changed cells, and whether it supports or eliminates each live hypothesis. Do not request another demo reflexively: state the concrete competing rules and the observable feature another pair could distinguish. If demos are costly and a returned pair does not materially narrow the candidate set, avoid spending further requests without a specific discriminating question.

This practice is especially important when examples have radically different sizes or appearances: compare them structurally and test candidate rules against all available pairs rather than repeatedly probing without synthesis.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/demo-chain-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/ray-inference-failure.md", "content": "# Ray extension inference after failed submissions

When a ray-extension prediction is rejected, reject the entire prior generator instead of making a cosmetic grid edit. Re-derive each ray independently from the demos: identify its seed/anchor coordinates, direction vector, color, whether the seed is part of the ray, and the stopping boundary. Track coexisting rays separately; one demo's ray color or direction must not be generalized to all marker groups without evidence. For a test with multiple potential ray seeds, enumerate candidate rays and validate each against every demo, including unchanged markers and endpoints. If the test has a configuration not represented in demonstrations, spend a demo only when it distinguishes explicitly named alternatives. Validate that generated cells do not overwrite given objects unless outputs prove overwrite behavior.
